   // concurrent rework legs — 08:04Z PR-armed (run titled 'workflow_dispatch # rework',
   // no anchor token) + 08:09Z issue-armed; the first leg's push moved the head
   // (c2e0d28f → 94988905) so the (pr, head) latch keyed a new head and never saw
   // the second arm coming.

   var BRANCH = 'ai/gh-100';
   var HEAD_OLD = 'c2e0d28f000';
   var HEAD_NEW = '949889050000';
   var LEG_PATH = '.github/workflows/ai-teammate.yml';

   function reworkItems() — per-rule items fn: issue item gh-100 + pr item pr-1017, both branch ai/gh-100, headSha HEAD_NEW.

   Rules: deployedRules(['rework-on-red-ci', 'rework-on-label']).
```

Wait — but in the both-armed same-tick scenario with deployed rules: rework-on-red-ci has `notPrLabels: ['agent:rework']` — so if the PR carries agent:rework, the issue rule defers. In the gh-1028 live case, the second (issue-armed) dispatch happened because at 08:09 the PR label had been consumed by the 08:04 dispatch, and the issue label was armed by fail_validation. So the sequence spans ticks: tick N: PR armed → dispatch → PR label consumed; tick N+1: head moved (leg pushed), validation red → fail_validation arms issue + consumes... wait no, fail_validation's issue arm adds agent:rework to the issue. Tick N+1's dispatch rules: rework-on-red-ci matches (issue armed, PR not armed) → dispatches leg 2.

So for the test: single rule `rework-on-red-ci`, item = issue gh-100 with prNumber 1017, branch ai/gh-100, head HEAD_NEW. workflowRuns.in_progress contains the FIRST leg's run: `{status:'in_progress', path: LEG_PATH, head_branch: BRANCH, head_sha: HEAD_OLD}` — different head. The title-token guards see nothing (no display_title/name anchor). Without the fix: dispatch fires (BUG). With the fix: suppressed. Assert capturedTriggers.length === 0. — This is the core AC test.

AC1 test (both arms, exactly one leg): simulate two ticks where the dispatch materializes a run. Use onTrigger to append the run into workflowRuns.in_progress (mock reads the object live per call). Tick structure: rules [rework-on-red-ci, rework-on-label], items fn: for the issue rule return issue item with labels ['agent:rework'] and pr labels [] (so notPrLabels passes); for the pr rule return pr item with labels ['agent:rework']. onTrigger pushes the leg run (head HEAD_NEW... the dispatch happens on the branch; the run's head_sha would be the current head at dispatch; use HEAD_NEW for leg... wait — AC2 wants the run on a DIFFERENT head to suppress. In the two-tick flow both dispatches would be at the same head... but in the LIVE case the head moved between dispatches because the first leg pushed mid-flight. So: first dispatch at HEAD_OLD... hmm, actually simpler: the in-flight run created by the first dispatch is at HEAD_OLD (the head when the first leg started); the second arm probes with the item's CURRENT head = HEAD_NEW (moved). The guard must still match. My onTrigger appends a run with head_sha HEAD_OLD while items report HEAD_NEW — exactly the live shape.

But wait: for both rules to dispatch in one tick, the second rule's item must also match. rework-on-label: query pr labels [agent:rework] — the PR item needs labels ['agent:rework']. And rework-on-red-ci: notPrLabels agent:rework — the ISSUE item carries pr: {labels: [...]}. With the issue rule first: it dispatches (no runs yet), onTrigger appends run → the PR rule then probes → suppressed → capturedTriggers.length === 1. 

